  "slug": "disease-associated-dysbiosis-c485",
  "summary": "Preprints now link microbial signatures in gut, oral, and body-wide sites to pediatric IBD and Parkinson disease, with spatial and proteomic methods.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of microbial community measurements, rather than host signals alone, to distinguish disease states. Metaproteomic microbial-protein panels from the ascending colon outperformed human-protein panels in machine-learning diagnosis of pediatric inflammatory bowel disease. In Parkinson disease, stool and plasma profiles from 211 patients and 204 controls showed dysbiosis-linked differences tied to diagnosis and severity, and a five-site study of 150 participants reported weakening cross-site microbial coordination and reduced core taxa alongside cognitive impairment. MiSA offers a spatial statistical route to detect disease-linked dysbiosis in image-based oral biofilm data from peri-implant mucositis. The NCF1 analysis addresses a host genetic question in chronic granulomatous disease and is separate from the microbial thread. Novelty lies in spatial and multisite resolution and in proteomic readouts for disease classification.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
